        lesson(
            "n4.7.2", "Suffering passive", "had something (unwanted) happen to me",
            form: "Person affected + は + doer + に + (object を) + passive verb; also works with verbs that take no object (雨に降られる, 子どもに泣かれる)",
            note: "Japanese uses the passive to say something happened that you didn't want, even with verbs that have no object: 雨に降られた (I got rained on), 夜中に赤ちゃんに泣かれた (the baby cried on me all night). The doer takes に and the thing affected keeps を: 隣の人にたばこを吸われた, 財布を盗まれた. Compare: 弟が私のケーキを食べた just states a fact; 弟にケーキを食べられた says you're upset about it. If someone did you a favor, use 〜てもらう instead — 先輩にレポートを見られた sounds like you didn't want them to look.",
