            note: "One form, two meanings — make someone do it, or let them do it — and context decides: 子どもに野菜を食べさせる (make the kids eat vegetables), 子どもを自由に遊ばせる (let the kids play as they like). If the verb already has a を object, the causee takes に so there aren't two を: 学生に作文を書かせる. With a verb that has no object, を is the default and sounds more forced, while に suggests letting them do what they wanted. To make \"letting\" clearly kind, add あげる／くれる: 行かせてあげる, 休ませてくれた. Use it for people below you or equals; asking a superior with させる is rude — use 〜ていただけますか.",
