            note: "I was made to do it, and didn't want to: 子どものころ、母に野菜を食べさせられた. The person forced is the subject, and whoever did the forcing takes に. Compare 〜させる (I made someone do it) and the plain passive (something was done to me). With う-verbs the short form 〜される is very common in speech (飲まされた, 待たされた), but not with verbs ending in す (話させられた, never 話さされた) or with る-verbs. It always sounds reluctant; if you were glad, say 〜させてもらった (I was allowed to).",
